A||N||5||TwisT||Need advice with using fiberglass gelcoat||||1||
Z||000000||TwisT||02-22-2001||01:30 PM||||Hey gang,<P>I have a friend at work who needs to do some fiberglass repairs.  He has a huge bucket of gelcoat,  but no instructions on how to use it.  We're also wondering if it needs a catylist to work.<P>I know a few of us Fiero enthusiasts are body men...  I'm not one of them.  =)<P>Can anybody help?  Thanks!<P>Brett ||||reg||1
Z||000001||ray b||02-22-2001||03:06 PM||||gel-coat does need catlist [mek], also needs to be in a mold ,can't be used for repare as must be away from air to kick off [get hard].<BR>used for new parts only as no wax in it to seal out air and can't be used on top of FG<BR>as wax in FG will prevent bond.<BR>as FG kicks off wax in it rizes to top and seals out air thats why you must sand FG between layers to get bond by removing wax.<BR>gel-coat in mold is put in first on bottom of mold then FG over it and wax in FG rizes<BR>to seal whole part at once.thats the only way to use gel-coat.<BR>I have done a lot of glass work on sail boats.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||02-22-2001||04:15 PM||||Quick tip on non waxed resin- to get the outer layer to set up to a non sticky finish just spray it with a cheap spray paint (seals out the air)  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||02-22-2001||04:34 PM||||Gelcoat is nothing more than resin with thickeners added. Gelcoat will be too thick to fully saturate the fiberglass reinforcement. Styrene can be added to thin the gelcoat, but it will increase the drying time. The wax is not in the fiberglass, it's in the resin. Resin can be purchased with or without the wax already added. Surfacing wax can be added to the top layer so that it will fully cure. MEKP is the proper catalyst for polyester resins and gelcoats. If it is Epoxy resin/gelcoat it's a whole different story. If the bucket of gelcoat is not labeled, you'll be doing a lot of guesswork.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||TwisT||02-22-2001||06:19 PM||||Wow,  now THIS is why I post my questions in the Pennock Forum!<P>Thanks for the input gang...  Looks like I need to tell the guy I work with to forget about the gelcoat thing.  Not even sure he wanted to,  other than the fact he has a big honkin' bucket of it.  I had a hunch he wouldn't be able to use it and it looks like I'm right!<P>Thanks again!<P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sage||02-22-2001||09:24 PM||||You can also thin g/c with acetone or a very hot laquer thinner.  This will allow you to spray it through a regular spray gun.  You thin it about 50/50 with the thinner, then add the mekp right before you use it.  You have to add about 4 or 5 times the amount you would to resin.  Immediately after spraying the g/c, spray on pva(poly vinyl alcohol) to seal it so it will kick.  Or like Phil said, you can use a cheap spray bomb.  It cures with the absence of air, so it just has to be sealed.  Or you can mix it like you would to put in a mold,(no thinning), and brush it on, then seal it with pva, and after it is kicked, sand with progressivly finer grits starting with 180 and working up to buffing compound.  This is how I put a finish on a plug before making a mold.  It is about the only finish that pretty much gurantees(sp?) that the mold won't "marry" the plug anywhere.<P>This works for me.<P>Just my .02<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
